::ENG3_U06_L03_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which focus is best for presenting literary research? {
~I will talk about a story and some sources.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=This presentation explains how recurring window imagery reveals the narrator's desire for escape.#Correct. This is a focused literary interpretation.
~There are many things in literature.#Incorrect. This is too broad.
~My sources are interesting.#Incorrect. This does not state an interpretation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Context: Which choice best synthesizes research and text evidence? {
~The research source is listed but never connected.#Incorrect. Listing is not synthesis.
~The text quote appears without explanation.#Incorrect. Evidence needs analysis.
~The speaker gives an opinion with no evidence.#Incorrect. Research presentation needs support.
=The speaker connects a text detail to a source idea to support one interpretation.#Correct. Synthesis combines evidence toward a claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Context: A student presents a literary claim. What should credible evidence do? {
=Support the claim with a relevant text detail or source idea.#Correct. Evidence should directly support the claim.
~Replace the claim entirely.#Incorrect. Evidence supports a claim; it does not replace it.
~Introduce an unrelated topic.#Incorrect. This breaks focus.
~Confuse the audience with no explanation.#Incorrect. Evidence needs explanation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: Which oral explanation best presents complex analysis? {
~The text has stuff that means things.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~I found sources and they are there.#Incorrect. This does not explain analysis.
=The repeated doorway image matters because it appears whenever the narrator must choose between comfort and change.#Correct. This explains a complex relationship clearly.
~The analysis is too complex to explain.#Incorrect. The goal is to make it understandable.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which presentation order is strongest? {
~Research source, unrelated image, claim, no explanation.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
=Claim, primary evidence, research support, explanation, closing.#Correct. This sequence supports listener understanding.
~Closing, source list, claim, evidence.#Incorrect. The closing should not begin the presentation.
~Evidence, evidence, evidence, no claim.#Incorrect. Listeners need a clear claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Context: A research question is too broad: "What is literature?" What is a better refined question? {
=How does doorway imagery in the story develop the narrator's conflict?#Correct. This question is focused and text-specific.
~Why do books exist?#Incorrect. This is still too broad.
~What are all symbols ever written?#Incorrect. This is impossible to research well.
~Is reading sometimes hard?#Incorrect. This is too general for literary research.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Context: Which evidence explanation is strongest? {
~This quote is good because it is good.#Incorrect. This is circular.
~The quote exists in the text.#Incorrect. This does not explain meaning.
~Research is important in many ways.#Incorrect. This is too general.
=The quote supports the claim because the locked door shows the character's limited choices.#Correct. This connects evidence to claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: What makes an oral version of complex analysis effective? {
~It uses every sentence from the essay.#Incorrect. Oral delivery should be adapted.
~It removes evidence to make the talk shorter.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed.
=It breaks the analysis into clear parts listeners can follow.#Correct. Organization supports audience understanding.
~It avoids explaining the claim.#Incorrect. Explanation is necessary.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which transition helps listeners connect research to analysis? {
~Anyway, moving on to something else.#Incorrect. This weakens connection.
=This source helps explain the pattern in the text because...#Correct. This transition connects research to analysis.
~Here is a random source.#Incorrect. This does not show connection.
~I forgot why this matters.#Incorrect. This weakens credibility.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Context: Which source use is most appropriate in an oral literary research presentation? {
=Paraphrase the source and explain how it supports the interpretation.#Correct. This is listener-friendly and analytical.
~Read a long source paragraph without explanation.#Incorrect. This can overload listeners.
~Mention a source title with no connection.#Incorrect. Sources need purpose.
~Use a source that contradicts the claim without addressing it.#Incorrect. Contradictory evidence needs explanation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: A speaker has three source ideas but only one supports the claim. What should the speaker do? {
~Use all three because more sources are always better.#Incorrect. Relevance matters.
~Use none of the sources.#Incorrect. One source is useful.
=Use the source idea that directly supports the claim.#Correct. Evidence should serve the focus.
~Change the presentation to avoid evidence.#Incorrect. The standard requires credible evidence.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Context: Which statement is a literary analysis claim? {
~The story was published in a book.#Incorrect. This is a fact, not analysis.
=The storm imagery reveals the character's fear of change.#Correct. This interprets a literary element.
~The story has pages.#Incorrect. This is not analysis.
~The source is online.#Incorrect. This is source location, not literary analysis.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: Which tone is best for presenting complex analysis? {
=Clear, formal, and audience-aware.#Correct. Complex analysis needs an appropriate tone.
~Sarcastic and dismissive.#Incorrect. This weakens academic presentation.
~Confusing and rushed.#Incorrect. This harms audience understanding.
~Casual with unrelated jokes.#Incorrect. This distracts from analysis.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Context: What should a speaker do when research evidence is complex? {
~Skip the evidence completely.#Incorrect. Evidence may be needed.
~Read it word for word quickly.#Incorrect. This overloads listeners.
~Use it without naming its connection.#Incorrect. Connection matters.
=Paraphrase it accurately and connect it to the claim.#Correct. This supports oral understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which closing best synthesizes a literary research presentation? {
~That is all the sources I found.#Incorrect. This lists, but does not synthesize.
=The primary text and research together show that the window image develops the narrator's longing for freedom.#Correct. This combines text and research around the claim.
~I talked for a while about a story.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~My presentation had quotes and slides.#Incorrect. This does not return to the interpretation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Context: Why should the speaker explain literary evidence after presenting it? {
=Listeners need to understand how the evidence supports the claim.#Correct. Explanation connects evidence to interpretation.
~Evidence automatically explains itself.#Incorrect. Evidence needs analysis.
~The speaker should avoid claims.#Incorrect. Claims guide analysis.
~Explanation makes evidence irrelevant.#Incorrect. Explanation strengthens evidence.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: A presentation uses complex terms without definitions. What should the speaker revise? {
~Add more undefined terms.#Incorrect. That increases confusion.
=Define key terms briefly for the audience.#Correct. This improves audience understanding.
~Remove the claim and keep the terms.#Incorrect. The claim is needed.
~Make the presentation faster.#Incorrect. Speed does not clarify terms.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Context: Which action shows research scope has been refined? {
~The student studies every American text ever written.#Incorrect. This is too broad.
~The student searches random topics.#Incorrect. This lacks focus.
=The student narrows from symbolism in literature to doorway imagery in one text.#Correct. This refines the scope.
~The student removes the research question completely.#Incorrect. Research needs a question.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which oral delivery choice helps listeners follow research evidence? {
=Introduce the source, paraphrase the idea, and explain its connection.#Correct. This sequence supports understanding.
~Name five sources with no explanation.#Incorrect. This becomes a list.
~Hide the source so the audience cannot evaluate it.#Incorrect. Credibility matters.
~Read source notes as fast as possible.#Incorrect. This overloads listeners.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Context: Which explanation connects evidence to theme? {
~The quote is in the text.#Incorrect. This does not explain theme.
=The repeated silence supports the theme of isolation because it shows how the character is cut off from others.#Correct. This connects evidence to theme.
~The theme is theme.#Incorrect. This is circular.
~The source is long.#Incorrect. Length does not explain theme.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.W.03] Context: Which revision improves audience understanding of complex analysis? {
~Add three unrelated claims.#Incorrect. This weakens focus.
~Use a longer sentence with more jargon.#Incorrect. This may confuse listeners.
=Break the point into two shorter sentences with a clear transition.#Correct. This makes analysis easier to follow.
~Remove all explanation.#Incorrect. Explanation is necessary.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.RS.01] Context: Which evidence choice best supports a claim about historical context? {
~A random modern advertisement.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
=A credible source explaining the time period connected to the text.#Correct. This supports historical context.
~A personal memory unrelated to the text.#Incorrect. This is not research support.
~A dictionary entry for an unrelated word.#Incorrect. This does not support the claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: What should a speaker avoid when presenting literary research? {
~Explaining how evidence supports a claim.#Incorrect. That is needed.
~Using a clear focus.#Incorrect. That is needed.
=Listing sources without explaining their relevance.#Correct. A list of sources is not a strong presentation.
~Paraphrasing complex source information.#Incorrect. Paraphrasing can help listeners.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.W.02] Context: Which claim is supported by evidence about repeated locked doors? {
=The locked doors symbolize the character's limited freedom.#Correct. The claim connects evidence to meaning.
~The story contains no symbols.#Incorrect. This contradicts the evidence.
~The doors prove the text is nonfiction.#Incorrect. This does not follow.
~The character never faces conflict.#Incorrect. Locked doors suggest conflict or limitation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L03_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: What is the best final readiness question before presenting literary research? {
~Did I include every source I found?#Incorrect. More sources are not always better.
=Can listeners follow my claim, evidence, research support, and explanation?#Correct. This checks organization and audience understanding.
~Did I make the slides look crowded?#Incorrect. Crowded slides hurt understanding.
~Can I skip explaining my interpretation?#Incorrect. Interpretation must be explained.
}
